@X0A
There is a common problem with dropping carrier while shelling out to dos
programs under OS/2.  This document will explain why this happens and some
possible solutions.


@X0BTHE CAUSE
---------
@X03Carrier is dropped when your serial driver detects that carrier has dropped.
When PCBoard shells to a dos program, it closes the comm port but DTR is not
dropped.  This is because PCBoard holds the DTR up.  It expects the dos window
to open the port again before DTR drops.

If the software that is being shelled to does not open the port in time,
carrier may be lost.

When the dos program exits, the port closes again, and PCBoard will pick it up.
if DTR is dropped, again, carrier loss will occur.  The problem is that most
applications don't have a STICKY DTR feature to hold the DTR up like PCBoard
does.



@X0BUSING A FOSSIL DRIVER
---------------------
@X03Since your fossil driver is usually unique to your serial driver and your
serial driver is unique to your serial ports, this may mean changing serial
cards or serial drivers.

When using the SIO driver, there is fossil support available and it will work
with most non-intelligent UART-type serial ports, which means that you need not
replace any serial ports.

SIO.SYS was written by Ray Gwinn and replaces your default OS/2 serial drivers.
There is a shareware version of SIO on Salt Air (@X0CSIO153.ZIP@X03) which is limited
to 4 ports.

To install SIO, run the install program and let it update your CONFIG.SYS.
When the program asks if you are running a HAYES ESP semi-intelligent card,
it is referring to a multi-port serial board from Hayes.  You'll know if you're
running one.  Most users must answer no.

When it is done, edit your CONFIG.SYS file.  Toward the bottom, look for:

@X0FDEVICE=C:\PATH\SIO.SYS
DEVICE=C:\PATH\VSIO.SYS

@X03Now insert a line just below this which says:

@X0FDEVICE=C:\PATH\VX00.SYS

@X03and that will install your fossil layer.  To ensure that there are no errors,
open a dos window on your desktop.  You should see the VX00 fossil driver
information at the top of the screen.  If you don't, rename your AUTOEXEC.BAT,
close the window, and try again in a new dos window.  Check for any errors on
this screen.  When you're done, be sure to replace your autoexec.bat with your
backup.


@X0BSTICKY DTR
----------
@X03PCBoard holds the DTR up with a sticky DTR switch.  To turn this switch on, run
@X02PCBSETUP@X03.  Select @X02MODEM INFORMATION @X03and then select @X02CONFIGURATION SWITCHES@X03.
There you'll find a setting which says @X02LEAVE DTR UP AT DROP TO DOS:@X03 where you
need to enter <Y>es.

You may find that the carrier drop occurs when RETURNING to PCBoard from a
shell.  This is because the software that is running in the batch file does NOT
have a sticky DTR function.  To solve this, replace it with a program which
DOES have sticky DTR.
